WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.976
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.892 --> 00:00:34.850
ZISL SLEPOVITCH: Hello.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.100
My name is Zisl
Slepovitch and I'm

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.400
musician in residence at Yale
University's Fortunoff Video

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.130
Archive for Holocaust
Testimonies.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:46.610
This video series is designed
as a companion to our album

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.550
Where is our Homeland?,
which is the first volume

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.040
in the Archive's ongoing
effort to present

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.670
songs and poems performed
by survivors in testimonies

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:59.990
given to the Fortunoff Archive
over the last 40 years.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.640
You can learn more
about the project

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:06.425
on the Archive's website
songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:06.425 --> 00:01:07.940
Where is our Homeland?

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:12.490
Songs from Testimonies:
Volume 1 was recorded in 2019

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:16.790
by the Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble
featuring renowned singer Sasha

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.610
Lurje, Craig Judelman
on the violin,

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:23.130
Joshua Camp on the accordion,
keyboards, and guitar,

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.800
Dmitry Ishenko on the bass,
and myself on woodwinds.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.900
The songs in this
collection differ vastly

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.380
in terms of style, social
and historical background,

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.730
language, and many
other aspects.

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:39.320
But collectively, they create
a multidimensional picture

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:44.440
of Jewish life before
and during World War II.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.630
One of the most musically and
ethnographically compelling

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.300
testimonies in the
Fortunoff Video Archive

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.200
is the one given by Jack M.,
a native of the Polish town

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.490
of Dzialoszyce.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.710
Jack had an impressive
memory and was

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:00.260
a great storyteller and singer.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.330
It is noteworthy
that Jack was also

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:05.120
recorded for The Folk
Song Project carried out

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.830
in the 1980s.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:10.190
Jack remembered really a wealth
of the pre-war traditional

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:13.900
Jewish repertoire, including
badkhones wedding jesters,

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.780
recitatives, folk songs of
all kinds, songs in Polish,

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.750
some of which he learned
while serving in the army.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.000
One of the most
mesmerizing Yiddish songs

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.990
Jack sang on his
testimony was Trayb di

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:29.540
khvalyes "Drive Your
Waves Deep River."

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.450
It is a song performed
on behalf of a woman who

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.840
is longing for her beloved
on the bank of a deep river.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.650
And she asks the river to tell
him about her bitter fate.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.260
This is a pagan Slavic
trait, which is also

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.270
referred to as folk Judaism.

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:47.120
Superstitions and dialogue
with the forces of nature

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.360
and other borrowings from
the para-religious beliefs

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.900
of the Slavic, Baltic, Finnish,
Moldavian, and Romanian

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:57.710
neighbors took their firm
place in the Jewish folklore,

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.810
which shouldn't come
as a surprise given

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:03.440
the 650-year long
history of coexistence.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.870
And this song is
not an exception.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:09.230
The melody is typical for the
Eastern Slavic rural lyrical

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.290
song of the 19th century,
with its characteristic

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:15.380
intrasyllabic vocalization
and wide vocal range.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:26.310
JACK M.: She is singing like
she was standing by the ocean

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:30.720
to the waves
instead of a letter.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.290
She gave the song like this.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:37.460
(SINGING) Drive your
waves, deep river

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.460
Drive them over mountains
and valleys

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.100
From afar, I send my beloved

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:52.020
A thousand greetings

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:54.420
She repeats this twice.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:59.860
(SINGING) Tell him, deep
river, Of my bitter fate.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.020
Of my bitter fate.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.020
For my life is like
a dismal valley,

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:13.780
Without a ray of sunlight

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.580
Those two I remember.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:22.500
But there was a third one,
but I can't remember it.

00:04:23.754 --> 00:04:33.660
[ENSEMBLE PLAYING]

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:18.540
SASHA LURJE: (SINGING)
Drive your waves, deep river

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:25.780
Drive them over mountains
and valleys

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:33.900
From afar, I send my beloved

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:40.060
A thousand greetings.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:49.340
From afar, I send my beloved

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:55.500
A thousand greetings.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:04.380
Tell him, deep river,

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:12.260
Of my bitter fate.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:19.900
For my life is like
a dismal valley,

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:27.460
Without a ray of sunlight.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:35.820
For my life is like
a dismal valley,

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:43.660
Without a ray of sunlight.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:57.300
[ENSEMBLE PLAYING]

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:36.740
Di ertsayl im, tifer taykh,

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:43.220
Fun mayn bitern shikzal,

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:53.260
Az mayn leybn iz vi ayn triber tul.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:08:00.780
On a zin un on shtral.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:09.900
Az mayn leybn iz vi ayn triber tul.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:25.420
On a zin un on shtral.

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:34.460
ELEONORE BIEZUNSKI:
Jack M. recalls

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:38.790
that he learned some of the
songs he knows from hoyfzinger

00:08:38.790 --> 00:08:43.900
and gassenzinger, courtyard
and street singers

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:47.110
who would come from
Warsaw to his town

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:51.020
and sing the latest hits.

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:53.600
But he learned this
particular song

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:57.350
from the balebosta
the female boss--

00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:01.550
in a shop where he was
apprenticing as a tailor.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:07.490
She would sing songs as she was
working, including lullabies.

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:11.300
In his testimony, Jack
compares this song

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:16.910
to a letter addressed to
a lover who has gone away.

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:19.970
He remembered two
verses of what must

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:23.790
have been a longer ballad,
comparable to the well-known

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:28.580
papier iz dokh veys,
or dortin dortin,

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:31.070
similar to other
Yiddish love songs

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.800
in the style of
this European genre,

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:38.720
reminiscent of the Russian
and German folk repertoires.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:41.780
The renowned
folklorist Ruth Rubin

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:49.160
wrote in her 1973 book Voices
of a People that, I quote,

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:52.940
"an examination of 19th
century Yiddish love songs

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:56.660
will reveal many parallels
with old German love

00:09:56.660 --> 00:10:00.020
songs of several centuries ago.

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:04.160
While other authors contain
only fragmentary evidence

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:10.070
of this early ancestry, the bulk
of Yiddish folk songs today,

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:13.910
however, relate mainly to
19th century life of the Jews

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:17.480
in the Czarist
Pale of Settlement.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:20.150
Within this framework,
these love songs

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:22.940
were the forerunners
which inspired

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:25.160
the first generations
of Yiddish lyric

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:30.920
poets in modern Yiddish
literature," end of quote.

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:35.300
As the co-coordinator of the
Ruth Rubin legacy website,

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:40.790
I have heard many such songs,
that you can also now access

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:48.290
easily since close to 2,000
songs have been digitized

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:53.180
and are now online as
part of this project.

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:57.020
It is notable that
this type of songs

00:10:57.020 --> 00:11:02.390
was often transmitted
through women who--

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:06.410
although they were not allowed
to perform publicly, mostly

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:09.080
because of religious reasons--

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:13.880
would sing abundantly
in their homes

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:20.860
and, like in the case of
Jack's balebosta at work.

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:26.320
The emotions of the songs
are conveyed through images

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:33.680
of nature in Trayb di khvalyes.

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:38.780
The mountains and valleys
represent the greetings

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:42.920
that the singer wishes to
send to their lost love.

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:47.660
At the same time, they
symbolize the very distance

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:51.560
separating the singer
from their loved ones,

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:54.950
while the waves can
be seen as reflecting

00:11:54.950 --> 00:12:00.080
the agitated emotional state
caused by their heartbreak

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:02.180
and longing.

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:06.560
As the joke goes, if you find
yourself in a Yiddish love

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:11.000
song, stay away from the river.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:13.760
The same could be said
of other European folk

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:16.880
songs, where desperate
lovers sometimes

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:20.450
ends up drowning themselves.

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:25.940
Although this song doesn't
present such a tragic ending,

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:30.320
the topography of the
feelings in the last two lines

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:34.490
convey bitterness through
the idea of depth,

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:38.330
whether it is the
valley, the river,

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:41.660
or the image of darkness.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:43.940
In their arrangement
of this song,

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:47.690
the Zisl Slepovitch
Ensemble and Sasha Lurje

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:52.070
have created a texture with
drone-like accompaniment

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:57.950
and unison in the melody, which
emphasizes both the lyricism

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:04.640
of the text and the underlying
darkness expressed within.

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:06.710
It's a beautiful work.

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:10.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

